  "query_preview": "..The piece of a broad Pacific storm system that moved\nthrough Utah and southwest Wyoming late night is moving into\nCanada this evening as the southern branch moves into the Desert\nSouthwest. A few showers linger near the Utah/Idaho border in\nassociation with the first piece, but these are rapidly\ndiminishing as the shortwave continues north. Temperatures have\nbeen on the cool side today, with maxes running 5-10F below\nseasonal normals. \n\nThe southern branch of the low is on track to slowly move across\nArizona on Saturday before turning to the northwest and moving\ninto Colorado on Saturday. This will keep moisture and instability\nover southern Utah, southeast Utah in particularly, resulting in\nthe development of scattered showers and thunderstorms throughout\nthe day. Overall coverage and duration will be much less than what\nwe saw the past few days, but can't rule out some areas of flash \nflooding. Once the system starts tracking northeast, the threat of\nshowers will spread across eastern Utah, with the showers \nprimarily focused over the higher terrain east of I-15.\n\nWeak ridging is then expected to move over Utah during the day on\nMonday, bringing dry conditions with maxes near seasonal normals.\nHowever, the storm track over the Pacific coast will remain open\n(and remain that way throughout the week) in the absence of a\nPacific ridge to block incoming systems. Therefore, confidence is\nhigh that next week will be wetter than average as a series of\nstorm systems move onshore and track east. However, there remains\na good about of ensemble spread with regard to the timing and\ntrack of any particular storm system, and therefore confidence\nremains low with regard to the details about potential impacts for\nUtah and southwest Wyoming.",
